+alternative. Guarded importer namespaces use their derived namespace origin canonically, so an
+otherwise identical callable receiver does not become ambiguous merely because a later join
+materialized the same provenance. These are bounded source-analysis rules, not an assertion that
+arbitrary Python reachability or external mutation is modeled.
+
+An exact index into a known tuple is handled by the existing finite-selection transfer and its
+resulting importer still reaches inventory and registry validation. An unknown index into the
+same importer-bearing tuple remains unresolved and is refused. The refusal fixture derives its
+index from `len(())`, which is zero at runtime but has no exact analyzer key; a controlled module
+import verifies that the actual Python path reaches the selected importer.
+
+Eager list, set, and dictionary comprehensions carry their actual module execution environment
+separately from lexical target bindings. A selected source helper may transfer its arguments and
+import effect only when the existing implicit-dispatch intake and its bound explicit callees prove
+the helper effect; unknown calls, global/nonlocal writes, and unproven implicit effects refuse.
+Generator bodies are deferred, even though the first iterable is evaluated immediately; their
+source helpers are admitted as effect-free only for a bounded no-call, no-import, no-cross-write
+body with no unmodeled implicit dispatch.
+
