+
+### P31/P38/P40 instance: lexical scope and equivalent importer provenance
+
+**Diagnostic question.** Does a temporary comprehension target change the enclosing module
+binding, does a definite deletion become an opaque value, or does derived namespace provenance
+make the same callable appear to have two origins? Each can create a fail-closed refusal on a
+source path whose real binding is known. Conversely, treating a conditionally deleted or mixed
+importer as definitely absent can hide a live call.
+
+**Closure move.** Keep comprehension target and containing scopes distinct, represent definite
+deletion separately while retaining module builtin fallback, and canonicalize derived namespace
+provenance at binding construction. Preserve actual importer identities across all joins; do not
+weaken ambiguous or temporal effect boundaries. Exercise lexical shadows, known local members,
+module and local deletion, builtin fallback, same-origin and mixed-origin joins, conditional
+deletion, and an opaque-call/rebind refusal. A known tuple index is a finite-selection positive;
+an analyzer-unknown index is the unsupported-flow negative. The negative fixture must reach its
+import in real Python, rather than fail first on an unbound index; `len(())` supplies a concrete
+zero without an analyzer-exact key. Native and source-wide compatibility require separate
+execution; source-tool controls alone do not establish them.
+
+**R17 execution-context disposition.** Lexical identity cannot decide when a comprehension body
+runs or which module globals a selected helper resolves. Carry eager timing and the actual module
+environment separately from comprehension-local targets; generator bodies stay deferred while
+their first iterable remains eager. Existing source bindings and the implicit-effect intake admit
+only bounded source-helper effects. A single generic expression-child intake refuses every
+comprehension-owned assignment expression, including lambda-default and nested-display forms,
+because complete evaluation-order and enclosing-scope transfer is absent. This also refuses a
+harmless scalar assignment with no import sink; retain that false-positive falsifier. Lambda-body
+assignments are owned by the lambda and are not comprehension outer writes.
+
+The same expression-effect/order class extends outside comprehensions: a dict-display scanner
+that visits value before key can report a stale or unknown importer target when key evaluation
+changes state. A registry row with that unknown target can then match and validate green. Before
+scanning any dict-display pair, admit the swapped transfer only when either side is an immutable
+literal, or both are plain name reads; otherwise refuse. This is one pairwise commutativity proof,
+not a target-name list. It also refuses harmless no-sink pairs such as a name key and a known
+builtin call; retain that precision falsifier. Mapping spreads, arbitrary object hashing,
+external effects and the rest of Python evaluation semantics are not established by this proof.
+Run actual collector, registry, removal/restoration and controlled-Python comparisons; selected
+source controls do not establish source-wide or native clearance.
